  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 422 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge will continue to dominate our weather for\nthe first half of the week. Ensemble models have increased\nconfidence that air temperatures will remain in the low to mid\n90s F through Wednesday. Temperatures at 850mb increase to\n22-24C (above 95th climatological percentile) during this time,\nfurther supporting that warm surface temperatures will continue.\nAn approaching cold front, which will be discussed further\nlater, may bring some cloud cover into northeastern MO and west\ncentral IL that may mitigate the heat some on Wednesday. High\ndewpoints will also persist in the region continuing to cause\nheat index values to be 100-108F. Some relief from the heat is\nprojected after Wednesday, but to what magnitude remains\nuncertain. A longwave trough deepens over the Great Lakes region\nbringing a cold front that will move through the region, but the\nexact positioning and magnitude of this front is currently\nuncertain. The cold front will bring relief from the dangerous\nheat, but the exact magnitude of the cool down is uncertain.\nModel guidance has IQRs of 5-10 degrees for Thursday through\nFriday and 10-15 degrees for next weekend.\n\nThe cold front will also be the catalyst for multiple rounds of\nrain Wednesday through Friday across the region. Ascent from\nupper level divergence coupled with the forcing from the cold\nfront will interact with highly anomalous moisture values and\nwill introduce a threat for heavy rain and flash flooding. The\nconfidence of the totals and location of the heaviest rainfall\nis very uncertain due to the fronts varying position in\nensemble guidance. Cluster analysis of ensemble members shows\nthat the swath of heavy rain could occur anywhere between\nnorthern Arkansas and southern Iowa. Flash flooding will be a\nconcern where this swath of heavy rain materializes. This amount\nof rain would also threaten river flooding, particularly on\nsmaller creeks and streams, but the exact placement of the heavy\nrain will be crucial in determining where this occurs.",
